  "source_note": "Measured with hashed prototype scripts retained under runs/aes-masked-schedule-20260918/prototypes; tool promotion and byte-identical regeneration recorded separately.",
  "study": "Fixed-topology first-order HPC2 register-scheduling experiment",
  "hypothesis": "Can scheduling remove the mapped area penalty of public29-AND AES S-boxes while preserving their randomness and delay advantages over a comparably optimized classic32-AND baseline?",
  "outcome": "Not supported. Every scheduled29-AND circuit is larger and slower than the equally scheduled classic circuit. All keep29 rather than32 fresh bits per transaction.",
  "model": {
    "latency_cycles": 8,
    "fixed": "Input circuit XOR/XNOR/AND topology; first-order two-share HPC2 gadget; one independent fresh bit per AND per transaction supplied at cycle0; initiation interval1.",
    "variables": "Integer node cycles0..8 and a Boolean operand orientation per AND. XOR consumes inputs in its output cycle; HPC2 consumes its first operand one cycle before output, second two cycles before. Each source delay chain is shared across its consumers.",
    "objective": "2*sum(last_consumption_cycle-source_cycle) plus sum(AND_output_cycle-2). Output signals consumed in cycle8. Internal gadget registers and valid pipeline are fixed for a given topology and excluded.",
    "optimality_boundary": "OR-Tools CP-SAT reports OPTIMAL with matching objective/bound; emitted alignment-register counts were independently matched. This is not a proof of minimum mapped area, delay, arbitrary latency, gadget topology, XOR count, nonlinear count, or globally best S-box. No external solver proof certificate is supplied.",
